INT. BEEKEEPERS' CONVENTION - HOSPITALITY SUITE - NIGHT

A beige hotel function room hung with banners reading BUZZ 2026. Folding tables of honey jars, a punch bowl, a cardboard cut-out of a queen bee with a crown. Fluorescent light and carpet the colour of old tea.

LENKA PROCHAZKA-ODUYA, thirties, in a sage cardigan with a lanyard, arranges honey jars by shade of amber. BASHIR KAMANGA, thirties, in a bow tie, leans on the next table, pretending to read the label on a jar. Near the punch, her friend COLETTE VANTERPOOL, forties, laughs at something on her phone, and beside her MAGNUS EKSTROM, fifties, stirs the punch with a ladle and tastes it with a frown.

BASHIR
That's a clover.

LENKA
It's a buckwheat.

BASHIR
It's a clover that's had a hard life.

LENKA
Do you always correct strangers?

BASHIR
I'm not a stranger. I'm the one who sat behind you at the keynote.

LENKA
You were breathing on my neck.

BASHIR
I was taking notes.

COLETTE
(across the room)
Lenka! Come taste this!

LENKA
(to Bashir)
Excuse me.

MAGNUS
It needs more lemon.

COLETTE
It needs less lemon.

MAGNUS
It needs a different punch.

LENKA
(arriving)
What's the matter?

COLETTE
Magnus thinks he's a sommelier.

MAGNUS
I am a sommelier.

COLETTE
You're a retired dentist.

MAGNUS
I'm both.

BASHIR
(drifting over)
May I try?

MAGNUS
By all means.

Bashir dips a cup, sips, and gives a thoughtful nod.

BASHIR
Honey. Citrus. A touch of regret.

LENKA
That's the buckwheat.

COLETTE
(to Lenka, low)
He's cute.

LENKA
(low, to herself)
He's wrong about the clover.

COLETTE
Then forgive him.

LENKA
Never.

BASHIR
(overhearing)
I'll wear you down.

LENKA
You can try.

MAGNUS
This is the most romantic thing I have ever seen at a beekeepers' convention.

COLETTE
It's the only thing.

MAGNUS
And I've been to eleven.
